---
/**
 * The hero shared by 21 solution pages and 4 persona pages.
 *
 * It used to stack six things before the fold: an uppercase eyebrow pill, the
 * h1, a subtitle, a differentiator line, two CTAs, and a full simulated
 * terminal with a disclaimer apologising for being simulated. This is the same
 * cut `SPHomeHero.astro` already made for the homepage, applied to the other 25
 * pages: title and one sentence.
 *
 * What left, and why:
 *   - the eyebrow pill, which repeated the category in shouted caps and, at
 *     390px, showed a fragment of it;
 *   - the simulated terminal, the largest object above the fold on every one of
 *     these pages, which shipped its own disclaimer;
 *   - the differentiator line, a second subtitle;
 *   - both CTAs (operator decision, round 6): the hero states the claim, the
 *     black bottom CTA carries the action.
 *
 * Round 6 also brought the solution video INTO the hero: when a caller passes
 * children (SolutionPage does, PersonaPage does not), the hero becomes a
 * two-column grid: text on the reading start edge, video on the end edge.
 * DOM order is text-then-video, so the grid mirrors itself under RTL and the
 * single-column collapse leads with the title, not a dark rectangle.
 *
 * The height comes from the content. `min-height: 100dvh` with
 * `justify-content: center` is gone from `.sp-hero`: on a page whose hero is
 * three short elements it reserved a whole viewport to centre them in, which is
 * exactly the measurement that took the homepage hero apart.
 */
interface Props {
  hero: {
    title: string;
    subtitle: string;
  };
}

const { hero } = Astro.props;
const hasMedia = Astro.slots.has('default');
---

<section class:list={['sp-hero', hasMedia && 'sp-hero-split']}>
  <div class="sp-hero-content">
    <h1>{hero.title}</h1>
    <p class="sp-hero-sub">{hero.subtitle}</p>
  </div>
  {hasMedia && (
    <div class="sp-hero-media" id="video">
      <slot />
    </div>
  )}
</section>
